  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when randomly picking an integer between $45$ and $51.$\n\nII. The age of a randomly selected person (child or adult) in an elementary school.\n\nIII. The height of a basketball player in centimeters.",
  "worked_solution": "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of integers between $45$ and $51,$ and since the selection is made at random, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, as there are likely to be more children than adults in the school.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the height of a basketball player is a real number, so the support is not finite. Moreover, each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
